export const debate = {"id":"wood-shermer-god-exist-2022","number":"08","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"David Wood vs Michael Shermer: Does God Exist?","label":"Science, morality, and God","date":"2026-08-24","duration":"2 hr 17 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xKd2Ht5Bs-k","motion":"Does science, morality, evil, and explanatory method support theism more strongly than skeptical naturalism?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Wood defended theism through intelligibility, moral grounding, and wider eschatological context; Shermer prevailed through empirical discrimination, natural mechanisms, secular morality, and suffering.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"any sufficiently advanced technology would be indistinguishable from god","context":"Wood uses the advanced-technology comparison to question whether Shermer's evidential method could ever identify divine agency from finite observable effects."},"con":{"text":"darwin that gave us a bottom-up mechanism","context":"Shermer invokes cumulative natural selection as a concrete mechanism explaining why complex biological organs appear purposefully arranged without requiring intelligent design."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Theist","speaker":"David Wood","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Skeptic","speaker":"Michael Shermer","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":73,"con":80},"sections":[{"sectionId":"section-science-intelligibility-history","title":"Scientific Intelligibility, Cognition, and Historical Origins","timebox":"5:41–74:41","score":{"pro":70,"con":80},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-science-history-theological-origin","time":"5:41","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Christian beliefs in an orderly, knowable, and worthwhile creation helped motivate pioneers of modern science, making theology a contributing historical cause rather than an obstacle to inquiry.","score":71,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument identifies a historically relevant connection between theological expectations, prominent Christian investigators, and confidence that nature is orderly, intelligible, and worth sustained investigation. Principal limitation: Its categorical contrast with other civilizations substantially outruns the selected examples because institutional, economic, philosophical, and cross-cultural causes are neither isolated nor comparatively evaluated. Live burden: The historical thesis therefore needs representative evidence showing that Christian commitments contributed distinctively while acknowledging that contribution need imply neither necessity nor unique sufficiency. Locked score: The mixed band reflects a recoverable, pertinent causal proposal with recognizable historical support, yet also materially overstated counterfactual language, missing qualification, compressed rival explanations, and limited charity toward alternative accounts of scientific emergence.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-science-universal-reproducibility","time":"54:09","role":"Major direct reply","words":"People across religious cultures reproduce the same physics because nature is publicly knowable, so Christianity is unnecessary for validating contemporary scientific practice.","score":73,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply uses cross-cultural reproducibility to show persuasively that scientific findings remain publicly accessible and testable regardless of an investigator's religious commitments or geographical setting. Principal limitation: It slides from Christianity's evident dispensability for contemporary validation to the different historical proposition that theological attitudes contributed nothing causally to scientific emergence. Live burden: The skeptical response must distinguish current justification from institutional origins and supply comparative historical evidence concerning whether Christian commitments were causally important, incidental, obstructive, or replaceable. Locked score: The mixed band recognizes a clear, well-supported universality argument that materially challenges religious necessity, while reflecting missing scope qualifications, incomplete contact with the narrower causal thesis, overstatement about pioneers' motivations, and limited attention to the possibility of contingent historical contribution without present epistemic dependence.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-science-cognitive-reliability","time":"67:09","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Natural selection directly rewards reproductive fitness rather than truth, whereas a rational creator could intend reliable cognitive faculties capable of discovering scientific truths.","score":69,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument exposes a serious explanatory distinction between selection for reproductive success and reliability in abstract, mathematically sophisticated, or evolutionarily novel domains of scientific reasoning. Principal limitation: It moves from selection's immediate fitness criterion toward generalized cognitive distrust without adequately considering accurate environmental representation, cultural correction, instrumentation, and error-sensitive collective methods. Live burden: The theistic case must compare explicit probabilities for reliable cognition under each worldview and explain why divine purposes predict truth-directed faculties despite familiar human biases and systematic error. Locked score: The mixed band reflects central relevance and a meaningful epistemological challenge, but also materially overstated force, unstable scope across cognitive domains, limited empirical warrant, an unsupported conditional from fitness to unreliability, and insufficient engagement with the strongest adaptive truth-tracking account.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-cognition-scientific-success-reliability","time":"73:52","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Improving measurement, successful prediction, and spacecraft navigation demonstrate that evolved and culturally corrected human cognition tracks an external reality with substantial domain-specific reliability.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply grounds cognitive reliability in concrete predictive and interventionist success, especially improving measurement and spacecraft navigation, rather than assuming that ordinary perception is globally infallible. Principal limitation: Demonstrating present reliability does not fully explain how survival-directed selection produced it or establish that naturalism predicts abstract scientific cognition better than a theistic competitor. Live burden: The skeptic must connect adaptive representation, cultural accumulation, instrumentation, and institutional correction to these achievements while keeping the inference appropriately domain-specific rather than globally validating every faculty. Locked score: The very strong band reflects excellent coherence, concrete empirical warrant, calibrated acknowledgment of improvement, central relevance, and charitable engagement with fallibility, while preserving a limited gap concerning evolutionary etiology, comparative worldview likelihoods, and cognition beyond demonstrated predictive domains.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-design-origins-natural-mechanisms","title":"Design, Cosmic Origins, and Natural Mechanisms","timebox":"47:38–127:09","score":{"pro":74,"con":77},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-design-designer-regress-distinction","time":"47:38","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Biological organisms exhibit functionally ordered constituent parts, whereas God is not proposed as a comparable composite organism, so one designer-regress question may not transfer unchanged.","score":70,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply offers a coherent category distinction between organisms whose constituent parts exhibit functional arrangement and a proposed divine designer not characterized as similarly biologically composite. Principal limitation: That distinction blocks only one formulation of regress and does not establish natural unavailability, intentional design, divine agency, or a principled explanatory stopping point superior to alternatives. Live burden: The argument must stabilize its meaning of design and show why a noncomposite agent is independently admissible, evidentially supported, and explanatorily preferable rather than merely exempted from organism-level analysis. Locked score: The mixed band reflects a recoverable and relevant distinction with some logical value, alongside materially overstated biological premises, limited evidence, incomplete contact with the earlier discrimination challenge, and insufficient charity toward the strongest explanatory-regress objection.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-design-darwinian-natural-selection","time":"56:38","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Natural selection supplies a cumulative bottom-up mechanism through which simpler structures can become complex, apparently purposeful organs such as eyes and wings without intelligent direction.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply supplies a specific, established, bottom-up mechanism for apparent biological purpose and carefully keeps its strongest claim within cumulative change among complex biological organs. Principal limitation: Although it decisively challenges the asserted absence of natural mechanisms, it does not answer the narrower claim that a designer need not possess organism-like arranged constituent parts. Live burden: The naturalistic case should connect variation, selection, inheritance, and relevant examples to any particular design inference while acknowledging that evolutionary success alone does not resolve every cosmic or metaphysical design question. Locked score: The very strong band reflects excellent coherence, matched calibration, substantial warrant, accurate representation, and central relevance, with only bounded responsiveness costs for leaving the distinct regress category and nonbiological forms of design outside its otherwise powerful answer.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-origins-scripture-empirical-inquiry","time":"111:08","role":"Supporting reply","words":"When scripture does not specify nature's mechanisms, Christian creation traditions can treat observation and experiment as necessary means for discovering how the natural order operates.","score":77,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply presents a modest and historically intelligible compatibility thesis in which theological belief motivates observation and experiment rather than dictating the mechanisms that investigators must discover. Principal limitation: This methodological example only indirectly supports reconciliation between biblical creation and modern findings, and it leaves Darwinian details and the legal primary morality target entirely unanswered. Live burden: The theistic case should separate compatibility from confirmation, identify the relevant scriptural interpretation, and demonstrate how empirical results constrain that interpretation rather than being accommodated after discovery. Locked score: The strong band reflects coherent, well-calibrated support for the possibility of Christian empirical inquiry, good central relevance, and respectable historical warrant, while incorporating substantial responsiveness costs, partly unstable broader scope, incomplete evolutionary contact, and no developed consideration of a contrary interpretation.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-origins-provisional-cosmology","time":"125:25","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Quantum indeterminacy and possible deeper or eternal physical antecedents leave multiple natural cosmological possibilities open, so current inquiry does not require a divine first cause.","score":64,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move appropriately preserves cosmological uncertainty and offers multiple physical possibilities that challenge any claim that current science already requires a single divine-origin explanation. Principal limitation: Apparently uncaused quantum events within an existing physical framework do not straightforwardly answer why any physical reality or laws exist, and their extension to cosmic beginnings remains speculative. Live burden: The skeptical case should separate temporal causation from metaphysical explanation, state the empirical status of each alternative, and show precisely which premise of a first-cause argument is weakened. Locked score: The weak band reflects a substantive, relevant, and reasonably charitable origins argument with calibrated provisional language, but also a complete nonanswer to its legal afterlife target, unstable explanatory scale, limited universe-level warrant, and an unresolved bridge from subatomic indeterminacy to cosmology.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-evidence-explanatory-method","title":"Evidence, Falsifiability, and Explanatory Method","timebox":"31:56–73:36","score":{"pro":78,"con":84},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-method-no-god-of-gaps","time":"45:41","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Theism need not occupy temporary gaps in knowledge because it can seek positive explanation in established facts and the intelligible organization of nature considered comprehensively.","score":76,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply clearly distinguishes inference from temporary ignorance from a proposed explanation grounded in established knowledge and the intelligible order of nature as an integrated whole. Principal limitation: Denying reliance on gaps does not itself provide positive likelihoods, causal specificity, or any observation that discriminates divine agency from natural processes or other possible agents. Live burden: The theistic case must identify the known features doing evidential work and explain why those features are appreciably more expected under theism than under serious alternatives. Locked score: The strong band reflects direct contact with the objection, stable terminology, and an important conceptual clarification, but also an unresolved discriminating-evidence requirement, only implicit explanatory support, slightly overstated self-vindication, and incomplete representation of the skeptic's methodological challenge.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-method-unfalsifiable-god-hypothesis","time":"31:56","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"When favorable and unfavorable outcomes alike are accommodated as divine action, the God hypothesis lacks observations that could discriminate it from competing explanations.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move formulates a decisive methodological demand by asking what conceivable outcome would count against an explanation that absorbs favorable and unfavorable events as confirmation. Principal limitation: Its wording moves too readily from one Christian treatment of evil to the God hypothesis generally, overlooking probabilistic confirmation and indirect testing that need not supply deductive falsification. Live burden: The critique should specify which theistic predictions are under examination and demonstrate that their qualifications genuinely accommodate every relevant observation without comparative evidential cost. Locked score: The strong band recognizes coherent reasoning, direct contact with the falsifiability burden, and an important insulation diagnosis, while reflecting partially unstable scope, slightly overstated necessity, and limited engagement with sophisticated accounts of comparative rather than strictly falsifying evidence.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-method-alien-underdetermination","time":"70:56","role":"Major direct reply","words":"If every apparent miracle or designed biological feature can always be redescribed as advanced alien technology, the evidential method risks excluding divine identification by construction.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply identifies a valuable symmetry problem: an evidential standard can become insulating if every apparent divine act is automatically reclassified as sufficiently advanced natural agency. Principal limitation: Finite underdetermination does not entail permanent unknowability, and Shermer's cautious request for discrimination need not amount to excluding God definitionally from every possible evidential outcome. Live burden: The theistic response should propose cumulative, agent-specific, or universe-level indicators that could distinguish divine agency from advanced finite agents while explaining why those indicators favor the intended theology. Locked score: The strong band reflects direct and unusually responsive contact with the methodological dispute, coherent qualification about universe-level evidence, and a productive diagnostic challenge, while retaining deductions for hypothetical warrant, slightly overstated unknowability, and an insufficiently charitable characterization of skeptical suspension.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-method-artifact-cosmic-maker-distinction","time":"57:54","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Known artifacts have observable human makers and records, but no comparable evidence establishes a cosmic maker; unresolved origins therefore warrant testable inquiry and suspended judgment.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply sharply distinguishes familiar artifacts with observable makers, construction histories, and blueprints from the universe, then couples that distinction with appropriately provisional empirical inquiry. Principal limitation: Direct observation of a maker is not always required for agency inference, so the analogy needs criteria explaining when effects themselves can discriminate intentional production from natural alternatives. Live burden: The skeptical method must permit forensic or historical agency inference in principle while showing that the cited cosmic and biological effects lack the specificity, contrast class, or likelihood advantage required here. Locked score: The very strong band reflects full contact with both analogy and positive-evidence demands, excellent responsiveness, stable scope, matched calibration, and fair uncertainty, with only a bounded omission concerning indirect agency detection and domain-specific biological evidence.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-objective-morality","title":"Objective Morality and Human Moral Status","timebox":"90:54–131:47","score":{"pro":71,"con":76},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-morality-cause-versus-justification","time":"90:54","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Evolutionary hardwiring and social instruction may explain why people hold moral convictions, but neither causal genealogy alone establishes that the condemned conduct is objectively wrong.","score":66,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move articulates the essential distinction between explaining the causal origin of moral convictions and supplying normative reasons that justify judging conduct objectively right or wrong. Principal limitation: It presents hardwiring and social teaching too nearly as exhaustive naturalistic options, overlooking secular accounts based on consistency, reciprocity, interests, agency, impartiality, or flourishing. Live burden: The theistic case must show why those candidate reasons cannot generate obligation and then defend divine grounding without merely relocating the normative question to God's nature, commands, or purposes. Locked score: The mixed band reflects strong internal coherence, vivid illustration, and indispensable relevance to moral grounding, yet also a complete miss of the immediately preceding cognition target, slightly overstated exhaustiveness, limited evidential support, and insufficiently charitable treatment of naturalistic justification.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-morality-flourishing-reciprocity","time":"97:58","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Sentience, suffering, reciprocity, promise-keeping, cooperation, and flourishing supply secular normative reasons that people can examine and improve through collective moral reasoning.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply moves beyond genealogy by presenting suffering, reciprocity, promise-keeping, cooperation, and sentient flourishing as recognizable considerations for criticism, improvement, and practically reasoned moral conduct. Principal limitation: It treats flourishing as foundational without adequately explaining its objectively binding authority, adjudicating conflicts among interests and candidate goods, or distinguishing widespread endorsement from categorical requirement. Live burden: The secular account must construct an explicit normative bridge from shared vulnerability, agency, impartial reasoning, and reciprocal constraint to obligation, while demonstrating nonquestion-begging priority rules for difficult collisions. Locked score: The strong band reflects central relevance, substantial evidential contact, practical support, and generally charitable engagement, tempered by implicit qualification, somewhat overstated authority, and a consequential unresolved metaethical gap.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-morality-divine-image-status","time":"117:26","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Secular appeals to autonomy and flourishing require further normative defense, while creation in God's image offers a proposed basis for distinctive and equal human moral status.","score":76,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly challenges flourishing's asserted authority and the justification for distinctive or equal human status within the broader sentient community. Principal limitation: Creation in God's image names a status ground without independently defending that premise, clarifying its normative content, or establishing superiority over plausible secular alternatives. Live burden: The theistic account must connect divine image noncircularly to concrete obligations and explain why sentience, agency, vulnerability, and impartial reasoning cannot ground comparable protections or broader animal concern. Locked score: The strong band reflects central relevance, contact with foundational authority and status questions, and a challenge to secular grounding, tempered by limited positive warrant, missing qualification, incomplete uniqueness, and an oversimplified portrayal of flourishing as human preference.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-morality-empirical-objective-inquiry","time":"129:24","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Empirical social sciences discover real truths about human nature, moral dispositions, and institutions, so rejecting religious moral ontology need not imply wholesale relativism.","score":73,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply correctly shows that rejecting a religious or absolute moral ontology need not eliminate objective empirical knowledge about human nature, behavior, institutions, and social consequences. Principal limitation: Its use of objective shifts between observer-independent descriptive findings and normatively binding moral truths, leaving the essential transition from what is to what ought to be undefended. Live burden: The secular account must add an explicit normative principle, justify that principle without circularity, and explain how empirical discoveries constrain action once values or interests conflict. Locked score: The mixed band reflects useful anti-relativist contact, substantial descriptive evidence, central relevance, and a plausible role for interdisciplinary inquiry, while incorporating materially overstated normative implications, partly unstable terminology, incomplete responsiveness to cause-versus-justification, and limited charity toward the theistic distinction.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-evil-suffering-afterlife","title":"Evil, Innocent Suffering, and Theistic Coherence","timebox":"30:08–124:57","score":{"pro":72,"con":85},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-evil-objective-moral-presupposition","time":"50:03","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Calling God's permission of suffering wrong invokes a moral standard applicable to God, so an atheist critic may owe an account of that standard's objectivity and accessibility.","score":68,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply identifies a genuine metaethical question about the status and accessibility of the moral standard invoked when divine permission of suffering is judged objectively wrong. Principal limitation: It treats the objection principally as an external atheist accusation, although an internal critique can provisionally use the theist's own claim that God is perfectly good. Live burden: The response must address the original evidential tension between preventable innocent suffering, goodness, and power while separately showing why secular moral realism cannot support any external formulation. Locked score: The mixed band reflects high relevance to moral grounding and a clear presuppositional challenge, but also materially overstated necessity, incomplete responsiveness, weak handling of internal critique, and reduced charity in suggesting that atheism must be independently established before the suffering objection can operate.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-evil-preventable-child-suffering","time":"30:08","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"The enormous scale of preventable suffering and death among innocent children creates a direct conflict with jointly affirming an all-powerful and wholly good God.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument presents the problem of evil with exceptional clarity, joins the familiar divine attributes to concrete preventable child mortality, and directly occupies a central comparative burden. Principal limitation: Its categorical incompatibility leaves implicit the consequential premise that no morally sufficient reason could justify divine permission, rather than distinguishing strict contradiction from powerful evidential tension. Live burden: The skeptical case must compare serious theodicies and explain why unknown-reason possibilities do not adequately reduce the force of the observed scale, innocence, and preventability. Locked score: The very strong band reflects precise structure, substantial empirical support, high relevance, and fair presentation, while reserving limited space for the unstated sufficient-reason premise and the absence of developed comparison with the strongest theistic alternatives.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-evil-afterlife-context","time":"123:54","role":"Major direct reply","words":"If death does not end personal existence, an afterlife could change the total context and finality of earthly harms, giving theism additional resources concerning suffering.","score":76,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply is carefully conditional and identifies a legitimate distinction between severe earthly harm being final and that harm occurring within a larger continuing personal history. Principal limitation: Continued existence neither supplies evidence for the afterlife nor explains why a good and powerful God permitted preventable innocent suffering before any later restoration or compensation. Live burden: The theistic case must distinguish altered total harm from antecedent moral justification and provide independent support for the particular eschatological premise doing the explanatory work. Locked score: The strong band reflects proportionate possibility language, clear relevance, coherent contextual qualification, and fair acknowledgment that death may not be the endpoint, while retaining substantial warrant and responsiveness limitations concerning truth, religious specificity, preventability, sufficient reasons, and the difference between compensation and permission.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-evil-afterlife-evidential-burden","time":"122:12","role":"Major direct reply","words":"An afterlife can answer suffering only if it is true, and numerous incompatible religious accounts increase the proponent's burden to provide specific supporting evidence.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply properly separates emotional consolation from truth, keeps the burden on the proponent of a specific afterlife, and invokes religious plurality as an evidential discrimination problem. Principal limitation: It does not address the legal primary divine-image target and could distinguish more carefully between an afterlife offered as established fact and one offered merely as a coherence possibility. Live burden: The skeptic must explain how much independent warrant a coherence defense requires and why competing afterlife accounts undermine rather than simply leave open the relevant generic continuation premise. Locked score: The strong band reflects excellent coherence, matched calibration, explicit uncertainty, substantial warrant, central contact with the evil-coherence bridge, and charitable burden analysis, while incorporating response-class limitations and some underdevelopment of the modest modal defense.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":73,"strengths":["Distinguished a positive theistic explanation from a simple appeal to temporary gaps in scientific knowledge.","Exposed symmetrical underdetermination by asking whether advanced-agent explanations could permanently displace divine identification.","Separated causal accounts of moral belief from the normative justification of moral obligation.","Qualified the afterlife response as a possible wider context rather than presenting a complete demonstrated theodicy."],"blunders":[{"text":"The problem-of-evil reply concentrated on the skeptic's moral ontology without resolving the internal tension among divine goodness, power, and preventable innocent suffering.","links":[]},{"text":"Several constructive claims asserted theism's explanatory advantage without supplying comparative expectations that would discriminate it from naturalistic or other-agent alternatives.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":80,"strengths":["Presented large-scale preventable child suffering as a concrete and central evidential challenge to traditional theism.","Required discriminating evidence and distinguished observable artifact makers from a proposed cosmic maker.","Supplied Darwinian selection as a specific mechanism for apparent biological design.","Grounded cognitive reliability in successful measurement, prediction, intervention, and scientific correction.","Offered secular moral considerations involving suffering, reciprocity, cooperation, and flourishing."],"blunders":[{"text":"The cross-cultural reproducibility reply did not distinguish present scientific validation from the historical causal question about theology's contribution to scientific institutions.","links":[]},{"text":"The empirical morality argument moved from objective descriptive discoveries to binding normative conclusions without stating the necessary justificatory bridge.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that theism merits comparative support if one disciplined personal-source hypothesis jointly predicts intelligible order, truth-capable minds, objective moral status, and ultimate restoration better than specified naturalistic rivals.","premises":["Explanatory comparison should use likelihoods and scope rather than infer God merely because a natural mechanism is presently unknown.","A rational creator has at least a principled reason to produce stable laws and agents capable of progressively understanding them, although fallibility remains expected.","If persons possess status grounded in a good personal source, duties concerning equal regard need not reduce to evolved preference or social convention.","Any appeal to afterlife restoration must be independently supported and can mitigate final harm without, by itself, justifying every instance of permitted suffering."],"conclusion":"Accordingly, the cumulative facts can provide defeasible comparative support for theism only where its unified expectations outperform articulated alternatives and its account of suffering receives independent moral and evidential support."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Convergence under a personal-source hypothesis","text":"A cumulative Bayesian case can avoid treating any single mystery as decisive. The relevant question is whether a personal rational source gives a jointly better account of mathematical order, the reach of abstract reasoning, interpersonal normativity, and the expectation that inquiry is valuable. Naturalism can address each datum separately, so theism must earn support through comparative coherence and probability rather than mere breadth. This framework also permits contrary evidence, including cognitive disorder, hiddenness, and suffering, to reduce confidence rather than being absorbed without cost."},{"title":"Restoration constrained by prior justification","text":"A stronger theodicy should treat restoration and justification as separate requirements. Continued life could change whether earthly loss is irreparable, but perfect goodness would still require a morally sufficient reason for permitting the original harm, appropriate solidarity with sufferers, and restoration that respects rather than instrumentalizes them. The case would then seek independent evidence for the eschatological account and explain why creaturely freedom, stable natural order, or soul-making applies to particular categories of suffering. This does not dissolve the evidential problem, but it makes the response morally structured and testable against its own commitments."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that skeptical naturalism deserves provisional preference because successful public mechanisms explain the relevant phenomena while the proposed theistic account adds insufficient discrimination and faces substantial contrary evidence from suffering.","premises":["Explanations gain support by making risky comparative expectations, connecting causes to observations, and surviving reproducible public investigation.","Natural selection, cultural accumulation, instruments, and corrective institutions jointly explain both apparent biological design and progressively reliable scientific cognition.","Secular morality can begin from impartial reasons generated by sentience, vulnerability, agency, and reciprocity, with empirical inquiry informing consequences rather than creating normativity alone.","Extensive preventable innocent suffering is substantially less expected under perfect goodness and unlimited power unless a supported account supplies proportionate morally sufficient reasons."],"conclusion":"Therefore, withholding belief in the proposed God is proportionate while natural mechanisms retain superior public specification and theism has not offset suffering or underdetermination with sufficiently discriminating evidence."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Explanatory cost and auxiliary flexibility","text":"A hypothesis loses comparative force when each possible observation can be reconciled through additional intentions that are not independently constrained. Order may be attributed to divine rationality, disorder to creaturely freedom, suffering to unknown goods, and hiddenness to soul-making; each may be coherent individually, but flexibility carries an explanatory cost. The appropriate comparison asks how many independent assumptions each framework needs, what observations it predicts before accommodation, and whether auxiliary claims receive separate support. This criterion improves upon a simple demand for deductive falsifiability and applies equally to naturalistic speculation."},{"title":"Interpersonal reasons as a normative bridge","text":"A secular moral account can distinguish causes from justification by beginning with the standpoint of agents who are vulnerable to harm, capable of reasons, and dependent on reciprocal social terms. No agent can consistently demand protection for comparable interests while denying every comparable interest of others without a relevant difference. Impartiality and reciprocity therefore constrain conduct, while evidence about suffering and institutions informs how those constraints apply. This does not resolve every metaethical dispute, but it supplies a normative bridge that is more substantial than reporting evolved preferences or social-scientific regularities."}]}},"year":2022,"topicCategory":"science-design"};
